<chapter id="boxed_tuprec">
#title("Tuples and Records")

#para("\

A #emphasis("boxed") tuple/record is just a reference to
some location in memory where a flat tuple/record is stored.
Often the word #emphasis("unboxed") is also used to describe a
flat tuple/record. A boxed tuple/record is allocated on heap and it is
not linear, so it could only be reclaimed by garbage collection (GC).
This dialect of ATS2 has no GC, and the compiler rejects the construction
of a boxed tuple or record, for instance, #dyncode("'(1, 2)"):

<programlisting>
error(3): nonlinear heap allocation is not supported (no GC): a boxed tuple
is constructed (and could never be freed); use a flat tuple @(...) or a
datavtype instead.
</programlisting>

In this chapter, I show how flat tuples and records are used, and how a
record with certain read-only fields can be made from a datavtype (a
heap-allocated record that has to be freed).

")

#para("\

In the following code,
the types #stacode("abc_tup")
and #stacode("abc_rec") are for flat tuples and records, respectively:

#sats2xhtml('\
typedef
abc_tup = @(int, int, string) // for tuples
typedef
abc_rec = @{a=int, b=int, c=string} // for records
')

Note that a tuple is just a special record in the sense that the
labels in the tuple are fixed to be ranging from 0 to n-1, inclusive,
where n is the tuple length. In the case of #stacode("abc_tup"), the
three associated labels are 0, 1, and 2.

")

#para("\

The following code demonstrates how tuples and records can be created
and their components (i.e., fields) taken out. A flat tuple/record is
stored in a variable (here, a toplevel variable declared with
#keycode("var")), so its fields can be updated:

#dats2xhtml('\
var x_tup = @(0, 1, "2") : abc_tup
var x_rec = @{a=0, b=1, c="2"} : abc_rec
//
val () = assertloc(x_tup.0 = x_rec.a)
val () = assertloc(x_tup.1 = x_rec.b)
val () = assertloc(x_tup.2 = x_rec.c)
')

")#comment("para")

#para("\

Both #dyncode("x_tup") and #dyncode("x_rec") are variables, which are
mutable: their fields are left-values. In the following code, the first
field of the tuple and the field #dyncode("a") of the record are updated:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = x_tup.0 := 100 // *OKAY*: x_tup.0 is a left-value
val () = x_rec.a := 100 // *OKAY*: x_rec.a is a left-value
//
val () = assertloc(x_tup.0 = x_rec.a)
')

If a flat tuple/record is bound with #keycode("val") instead of
#keycode("var"), then its fields cannot be updated. A flat tuple/record can
also be passed to and returned from functions by value (a copy is made),
or by reference (a call-by-reference argument).

")#comment("para")

#para("\

If we want to have records of a type where certain fields are
read-only while the others may be updated, we can do this by making
use of the support for abstract types in ATS.  In the following
example, #stacode("myrec") is declared to be an abstract linear type; there
are three fields associated with #stacode("myrec") that are of the names
#dyncode("a"), #dyncode("b"), and #dyncode("c"); the first two may be
updated while the third one is read-only. Because a value of
#stacode("myrec") is linear, the interface contains a function
#dyncode("myrec_free"), and the other functions borrow the record
(the symbol #keycode("!") in front of the type):

#dats2xhtml('\
absvtype myrec = ptr  
//
extern
fun{}
myrec_make
(
  a: int, b: int, c: string
) : myrec // end-of-function
//
extern
fun{}
myrec_free : myrec -> void
//
extern
fun{}
myrec_get_a : (!myrec) -> int
extern
fun{}
myrec_set_a : (!myrec, int) -> void
extern
fun{}
myrec_get_b : (!myrec) -> int
extern
fun{}
myrec_set_b : (!myrec, int) -> void
extern
fun{}
myrec_get_c : (!myrec) -> string
//
overload .a with myrec_get_a
overload .a with myrec_set_a
overload .b with myrec_get_b
overload .b with myrec_set_b
overload .c with myrec_get_c
')

The implementation of #stacode("myrec") is a datavtype that
has a single constructor, so there is no tag in the representation of the
values; a field is accessed (or updated) by unfolding such a value in place
(the pattern with #keycode("@")) and folding it back:

#dats2xhtml('\
local
//
datavtype
myrec_rep = MYREC of (int, int, string)
//
assume myrec = myrec_rep
//
in (* in-of-local *)
//
implement
{}(*tmp*)
myrec_make
  (a, b, c) = MYREC(a, b, c)
implement
{}(*tmp*)
myrec_free
  (x) = let val~MYREC(_, _, _) = x in (*none*) end
//
implement{} myrec_get_a(x) = let val+MYREC(a, _, _) = x in a end
implement{} myrec_set_a(x, a) =
  let val+@MYREC(a0, _, _) = x in a0 := a; fold@(x) end
//
implement{} myrec_get_b(x) = let val+MYREC(_, b, _) = x in b end
implement{} myrec_set_b(x, b) =
  let val+@MYREC(_, b0, _) = x in b0 := b; fold@(x) end
//
implement{} myrec_get_c(x) = let val+MYREC(_, _, c) = x in c end
// there is no update for the c-field
//
end // end of [local]
')

")

#para("\

Following is some code that creates a
record of the type #stacode("myrec") and then
accesses and updates certain fields of the
created record:

#dats2xhtml('\
val x_abc = myrec_make(0, 1, "2")
//
val ((*void*)) = assertloc(x_abc.a() = 0)
val ((*void*)) = assertloc(x_abc.b() = 1)
val ((*void*)) = assertloc(x_abc.c() = "2")
//
val ((*void*)) = x_abc.a(100)
val ((*void*)) = assertloc(x_abc.a() = 100)
//
val ((*void*)) = x_abc.b(101)
val ((*void*)) = assertloc(x_abc.b() = 101)
//
val ((*void*)) = myrec_free(x_abc)
')

An attempt to update the field #dyncode("c") is a type-error as there is
no function for it, and if the last line is deleted, the compiler complains
that the linear variable #dyncode("x_abc") is not consumed.
While this example (#stacode("myrec")) demonstrates an approach
to constructing records containing both read-only fields and fields that
can be updated, this approach does seem a bit too verbose. One
possibilty is to develop meta-programming support so as to greatly
reduce the verbosity (due to the need for boilerplate code).

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_tuprec_1.dats", "on-line") the
entirety of the code used in this chapter plus some testing code.\

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"boxed_tuprec"-->

